vd2w&oe=6B0092EC", "width": 1080, "height": 1920, "id": "12", "resolution": "1080x1920"}], "comments": [{"author": "rinaldo.giammarino", "author_id": "10908443662", "id": "18183023359445967", "text": "The analysis of the 2026–2032 scenario is based on the Benner Cycle chart and offers an excellent starting point for exploring the relationship between historical cyclical analysis models and the contemporary macroeconomic reality.\nOver time, this framework has continued to hold a certain appeal for long-term-oriented investors. In short, what would it mean to apply this forecast today?\nThe geometric implications of the 2026 and 2032 models:\nLooking at Samuel Benner’s original structure:\n2026 (Category \"B\"): Represents the peak of a cycle. In Benner’s framework, the upper vertical lines indicate a period of \"high prices\" and \"good times\"—the ideal moment to monetize, take profits, and divest assets.\n2032 (Category \"C\"): Marks the nadir—a point of significant decline or economic depression. At this stage, prices fall, creating conditions to acquire assets at a discount in anticipation of a subsequent recovery.\nIf one were to look solely at the geometry of the chart, one would expect the stock market to lose momentum during 2026, followed by a multi-year period of attrition or correction, bottoming out in 2032.", "timestamp": 1794821552, "like_count": 0}, {"author": "eathdtravel", "author_id": "8047410288", "id": "18641483758012908", "text": "I called last time the next move on your Nov 7th reel where you have been saying something else. And no most likely we are not down with the downtrend", "timestamp": 1794810137, "like_count": 1}, {"author": "bjzilla17", "author_id": "209289039", "id": "17913397239578133", "text": "Bro a corrective ABC to the upside is bearish what do you mean reversal?", "timestamp": 1794809931, "like_count": 0}, {"author": "mykvo", "author_id": "7427952977", "id": "18039700406891653", "text": "🤫", "timestamp": 1794809207, "like_count"